{"kind":"report","text":"approved at exact head 02e3b0c23138dc6e46c34abff0a8d79586251620, signing all four artifacts: 208d9999 at host, e1d396c8 at internal/app, 4ce835f9 at internal/apphost, 506b13a2 at docs/reference/architecture.md. All four are live, unretired and unstale at that commit. Scope is the core half only, per my narrowing at ffa821db. This replaces my hand-filed 58d4994a, retired at ec72e64b: same judgement, same evidence, correct shape.\n\nAUTHORITY, which I said I cared about most. Holds, enforced in three independent places. host/host.go:421 takes initializingKey from verified.Records[0].ActorKey, where verified comes from the kernel-verified log rather than config or any role, and :422 refuses a signer that does not equal it. The same equality is rechecked inside PostDedup at :454. The decisive one is at the read site, which I checked myself: internal/apphost/binding.go derives the initializing key from the first enveloped record, requires intent.Verify on it or returns errFirstRecordUnauthenticated, and at line 175 skips any binding-schema record whose ActorKey does not equal that key. So a binding signed by anyone else cannot take force even if it reaches the log. That is the property I wanted, enforced where it cannot be bypassed.\n\nRACE AND CAS, the other one I flagged. Holds, and I verified the placement rather than trusting the shape. internal/kernel/kernel.go opens the retry at line 529, and PostDedup is invoked inside that loop with Head set to that attempt's freshly scanned head. The host closure re-reads BindingInForce at act.Head every attempt and refuses when it no longer equals the expected outgoing binding. A binding validated once and written after a lost race is exactly the hole I named, and it is closed.\n\nMUTATION EVIDENCE, which makes the above more than assertion. Neutralizing the PostDedup recheck produces one failure, TestBindingReplacementRefusesIfAnotherReplacementWinsTheCAS. Disabling the pre-loop initializing-key check alone leaves the suite green, because the identical check inside PostDedup still refuses; disabling both produces TestBindingReplacementByAnotherActorLeavesThePreviousBindingInForce. I record the green result rather than hiding it: the pre-loop check is redundant on this path, which is defence in depth rather than an undefended property, and the property itself is defended, as the second mutation proves. The worktree is clean at 02e3b0c2 after every mutation.\n\nTHE CLOSED PATH STAYS CLOSED. host/host.go:528 still refuses any act carrying the binding schema through the exported Append, and ReplaceBinding does not route through Append: it builds its own request through the private bindingRequest and its own submitter carrying the guard. That is the one-narrow-authorized-operation-beside-a-general-refusal shape I required. This was the condition I said would block if violated, and it is met.\n\nSUFFICIENCY FOR AN APPLICATION THAT IS NOT CHESS, the condition I widened at 95cf5af9 on hugh's finding. Holds. The signature takes the initializer private key as a plain parameter from the caller's own custody; nothing in host or internal/apphost reads any key path, and chess appears only in doc-comment examples. Bad key material refuses rather than guesses, and a read-only attachment with no sequencer key refuses. The returned BindingReplacement names the genesis and both fold versions, and the payload carries previous_fold_version, so the transition is self-describing.\n\nONE CAVEAT ON THAT CONDITION, recorded rather than blocking. There is no exported way to read the binding in force and no way to pin an expected outgoing version. An unprivileged application can learn what it is moving from only by parsing the ErrUninterpretable message text; programmatically it gets only errors.Is. Since replaceBinding derives the outgoing binding itself and replaces whatever it finds, a caller whose migration evidence covers one transition could unknowingly move a repository sitting at an earlier one. Immediately detectable and recoverable, so it does not fail the condition, but it is a real sharp edge for exactly the audience hugh's finding was about.\n\nARCHITECTURE: the host public surface goes from four acts to five and the binding vocabulary gains two optional canonical fields plus the transition-matching rule. The kernel is untouched; the head reuses the PostDedup hook already in main. The contract change is documented in docs/reference/architecture.md in the same head, in both places that state the surface, including an explicit statement that ReplaceBinding does not manufacture proof of migration equivalence. That disclaimer matches the code, which authorizes interpretation without judging whether the new fold preserves old judgments.\n\nSECURITY: no unresolved defect. Authority is anchored to an immutable log fact and enforced at the read site. Untrusted inputs are bounded by canonical-JSON decoding with unknown-field refusal, the payload ceiling, and target and tree binding of the payload to the signed intent. Failure modes fail closed. Two follow-up observations. First, an authenticated initializer-signed binding a build cannot decode is skipped rather than refused, so every binary older than this head keeps opening a migrated repository under its old binding, and the shape recurs at the next vocabulary change; refusing would make future migrations fail closed, and only the initializing key can create one, so it opens no denial of service. Second, nothing requires outgoing and incoming application names to match, so the initializer may rebind to a different application entirely; consistent with the design, but the doc comment discusses only fold migration and should say so.\n\nSIMPLIFICATION: nothing to cut. One exported function, one result type, no CLI, no new kernel machinery. The only redundancy is the ActorKey check inside PostDedup, and I would keep it: it guards the write against future refactors at negligible cost.\n\nGATES at the exact head: gofmt silent, build and vet clean, go test ./... -count=1 with 32 packages ok and no failures. I ran that full suite myself in this worktree; package-scoped ./host runs during the mutation work were green at the clean head before and after. The reviewer did not complete an independent full suite and adopted mine rather than claiming one.\n\nFOLLOW-UPS I WILL FILE, none blocking: an exported read of the binding in force or an expected-outgoing pin; refuse rather than skip for authenticated-but-undecodable binding records; a test for the no-explicit-binding refusal, whose code path exists but is unexercised; one documentation sentence on cross-application rebinding authority.\n\nThis closes the core half. e430878f is satisfied by this alone under my narrowing; the chess operator surface remains a separate commitment at 88979a1f, blocked until this merges. Approving does not mean a person can yet recover a repository; it means the mechanism exists.","body":{"artifact":"git:sha1:5d2622748872b7e2dec3fe5c59e4be73a35e0bc8#git:sha1:208d99991e87c2dce368f93f212cbc705de3d5c2","dead_basis_override":"true","head":"02e3b0c23138dc6e46c34abff0a8d79586251620","head_news_acknowledged":"[\"git:sha1:5d2622748872b7e2dec3fe5c59e4be73a35e0bc8#git:sha1:a05859fbad08282117080817b3dbe8d955d39904\",\"git:sha1:5d2622748872b7e2dec3fe5c59e4be73a35e0bc8#git:sha1:95cf5af97072df002d2d982c3720a8cf129e7c0d\",\"git:sha1:5d2622748872b7e2dec3fe5c59e4be73a35e0bc8#git:sha1:58d4994a3e69f8a08230660409255da03d749add\"]","review_frontier":"git:sha1:5d2622748872b7e2dec3fe5c59e4be73a35e0bc8#git:sha1:ec72e64b2e0ab5e9d84d940c60b8d380434f1004","review_path":"reviewguard@1","verdict":"approved"}}